// @emit-error .values() scratch frame not reserved
// FILED, PRE-EXISTING (the reservation-scan family): a BOUND `.values()` / `.keys()`
// list built off a DIRECT, `is`-narrowed DECLARED-union-arm map field does not
// reserve the key-list scratch frame, so the emitter fails loudly here rather than
// writing a module. The BOUND receiver twin — `const b = e.h; const vs = b.values()`
// — runs, which is what locates the gap in the receiver form the scan recognizes
// rather than in the op.
//
// It is NOT about `Set`s, and not about the key rep: this file's map is a plain
// `{[i32]: i32}` populated with `m[k] = v`, its `{[string]: i32}` twin fails
// identically, and the `.keys()` spelling fails the same way. It surfaced in the
// `.add`-through-a-field sweep only because that sweep was the first to run
// `.values()` at this receiver; the same cells fail on master with `.set`.
//
// The mechanism is `values-bound.vl`'s, one receiver over: the collect scan fires on
// every `.values()` CALL NODE including a `for v in m.values()` iterable that needs
// no frame, so the reservation cannot be widened from the call node alone — it has to
// tell the BOUND call from the for-in iterable first. Fixing that is the shared fix
// for the string-keyed Set `.values()` reject `values-bound.vl` already files.
type Hits = { h: {[i32]: i32} }
type Miss = { m: i32 }
type Ev = Hits | Miss

const e: Ev = { h: Map() }
if e is Hits {
  e.h[11] = 3
  e.h[22] = 4
  const vs = e.h.values()
  print(vs.length)
}
